<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kali Gandaki River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kali Gandaki is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> that rises near the Tibetan border in Mustang and flows south between the Dhaulagiri and Annapurna massifs through one of the deepest gorges in the world. Its valley was an old trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for salt and grain. Lower down it becomes the Gandaki, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kali-gandaki-river.jpg" alt="Kali Gandaki River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 南アジア · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narayani_bridge_in_Gaindakot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
